WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.896
Secrets.

2
00:00:01.176 --> 00:00:02.328
Keep three rules.

3
00:00:02.608 --> 00:00:13.339
Never put secrets in prompts: assume prompts are readable (LLM07), and remember that a prompt sent to a model provider is data you’ve shared with that provider.

4
00:00:13.619 --> 00:00:21.725
Never put secrets in code or committed config: load them from environment variables or a secret manager, as you did in week 1.

5
00:00:22.005 --> 00:00:28.043
OWASP’s secrets management cheat sheet adds rotation, least privilege per secret, and audit logging.

6
00:00:28.323 --> 00:00:37.069
GitHub’s secret scanning detects known credential formats in repositories and, with push protection, can block a commit that contains one.

7
00:00:37.349 --> 00:00:39.803
Turn it on for your project repository.

8
00:00:40.083 --> 00:00:51.411
Never log secrets or raw personal data: week 3’s redaction belongs in front of every log sink, and the lab’s PII patterns include API key formats for that reason.

9
00:00:51.691 --> 00:00:54.037
Two operational habits complete the picture.

10
00:00:54.317 --> 00:01:03.235
Use separate keys per environment (development, staging, production) with spending limits, so a leaked development key can’t run up a production bill.

11
00:01:03.515 --> 00:01:09.424
And know how to revoke and rotate each key quickly; you’ll put that in the incident runbook in lesson 12.
